## Phase 0: Environment Setup, Architecture Rules & Project Structure

Before creating a single character or room, establishing clean engineering foundations prevents 90% of crashes and messy refactors later.

### 1. Development Toolchain Setup

* **Engine & IDE:** Install Unreal Engine 5 alongside Visual Studio 2022 (or JetBrains Rider). If using Visual Studio, ensure the **Game Development with C++**, **Desktop Development with C++**, and the latest **Windows SDK** workloads are installed.
* **Compilation Discipline:** Unreal includes **Live Coding**, which is convenient for minor logic adjustments inside existing functions. However, whenever you create a new C++ class, modify class variables, alter constructors, or change inheritance hierarchies, always close the Unreal Editor and compile cleanly from your IDE before reopening the project. This prevents corrupted Blueprint assets and mysterious editor crashes.

### 2. The Golden Rule of Unreal Hybrid Architecture

Never try to force 100% of the game into C++, and never let gameplay logic sprawl into Blueprints. Divide responsibilities strictly:

* **C++ Owns Logic & Architecture:** All core base classes, state machines, mathematical calculations, procedural generation algorithms, AI controllers, interfaces, data structures, and save logic live in C++.
* **Blueprints Own Visuals & Tuning:** Every C++ gameplay class gets a child Blueprint class (for example, your C++ player character becomes a Blueprint player character). Inside Blueprints, you assign 3D meshes, skeletal animations, particle effects, sound cues, UI styling, and tweakable balance numbers (like health, speed, or damage).

### 3. Essential Unreal C++ Memory Concepts

Unreal manages memory differently from standard C++. Keep three core habits in mind from day one:

* **Reflection & Garbage Collection:** Unreal tracks objects created inside the engine and automatically deletes anything it believes is no longer referenced. Always expose object pointers and gameplay variables to Unreal's reflection system so the Garbage Collector knows they are still in use, otherwise your pointers will silently become invalid and crash the game.
* **Defensive Null Checking:** Before calling any function on a pointer—such as an AI target, a world reference, or a component—always verify it is valid. In fast-paced combat, actors die and get destroyed mid-frame.
* **Event-Driven Design Over Tick:** Avoid putting checks inside the per-frame **Tick** function wherever possible. Instead, use **Delegates** (event dispatchers) and **Timers**. Health bars should only update when damage actually happens; dungeon doors should only check lock states when an enemy dies.

### 4. Recommended Folder Structure

Keep both your **Source** directory (C++ files) and **Content** directory (Unreal assets) mirrored with identical domain folders:

* **Core:** GameMode, GameInstance, PlayerController, custom Interfaces.
* **Character:** Player character, Enemy base character, movement/dodge logic.
* **Components:** Health, Combat, Stats, Interaction.
* **Dungeon:** Dungeon Generator, Room Base, Doorways, Traps, Room Data Assets.
* **AI:** AI Controller, Behavior Trees, Blackboards, EQS Queries, Custom Tasks.
* **Progression:** Upgrades, Loot Pedestals, Data Tables, Difficulty Curves.
* **UI:** Base Widget classes, HUD, Menus, Damage Numbers.
* **SaveSystem:** SaveGame class and Save Management Subsystem.

