## Phase 6: Persistence (Save System), Polish ("Juice"), Optimization & Packaging

This final phase transforms a working technical prototype into a real, shippable standalone game.

### 1. Persistent Meta-Progression Save System

* **SaveGame Data Object:** Create a C++ **SaveGame** subclass storing only permanent data that survives closing the game: Meta-Currency balance, Unlocked Weapons, Permanent Stat Upgrade ranks, Highest Floor Reached, Total Bosses Defeated, and Audio/Graphics preferences.
* **Save Subsystem:** Manage saving and loading inside a **GameInstance Subsystem** so any class can request a save without hunting for references.
* **Async Saving & Checkpoints:** Always use **Asynchronous Save** functions so writing to the hard drive never causes a noticeable micro-freeze during gameplay. Trigger saves automatically at clean transitions: completing a floor, buying a permanent unlock, dying on a run, or changing settings.

### 2. Combat Feel & Game Polish ("Juice")

Good mechanics become unforgettable when paired with crisp sensory feedback driven directly from your C++ combat events:

* **Hit-Stop (Impact Freeze):** On heavy melee strikes or killing blows, briefly slow global time dilation (or pause animation play rate on the attacker and target) for 40 to 70 milliseconds. That split-second freeze sells the weight of a sword impact immediately.
* **Micro Camera Shake:** Trigger short, directional **Camera Shake** assets from the PlayerController scaled proportionally to damage dealt or taken.
* **Emissive Hit Flash:** Add a scalar **HitFlash** parameter to your enemy master material. Whenever an enemy takes damage, set that parameter to 1.0 in C++ and smoothly decay it back to 0.0 over 0.12 seconds using a timer or material curve.
* **Floating Damage Numbers:** Spawn lightweight pooled Widget Components or Niagara text sprites at the hit location showing damage dealt (larger and gold-tinted for Critical Hits).
* **Audio Layering & Concurrency:** Assign distinct Sound Cues for weapon swings, flesh hits, shield blocks, dodges, footsteps, room locks, and upgrade pickups. Configure **Sound Concurrency** rules so hitting five enemies simultaneously does not stack five identical loud impact sounds into distortion.

### 3. Profiling, Debugging & Stability Verification

Before packaging, run systematic stress tests inside the editor and in **Standalone Game** mode:

* **AI & Navigation Inspection:** Press the **Gameplay Debugger** hotkey (apostrophe key by default) while playing to view live Behavior Tree nodes, Blackboard values, Perception cones, and EQS scoring spheres right on top of enemies. Toggle NavMesh visualization (**P** key) in the viewport to verify doorways have unbroken green navigation paths across every stitched seam.
* **Performance Profiling:** Use console commands (**stat fps** and **stat unit**) to monitor Frame Time, Game Thread (C++ CPU work), Draw Thread, and GPU time. If Game Thread spikes during combat, check for excessive per-frame Tick functions or unpooled particle/projectile spawns.
* **Generator Stress Test:** Write a temporary debug loop that generates and destroys 50 different dungeon seeds back-to-back to verify zero infinite loops, zero unplaced Boss Rooms, and zero memory leaks.

### 4. Packaging & Shipping Build Checklist

1. **Configure Maps to Cook:** Open **Project Settings -> Packaging**, expand the advanced packaging options, and explicitly add every playable map (Main Menu, Hub, Dungeon Map) to **List of Maps to Include in a Packaged Build**. Missing this step causes packaged builds to launch into a black screen.
2. **Disable Debug Artifacts:** Wrap any debug draw lines, on-screen debug text messages, or cheat hotkeys in non-shipping build guards so they are stripped automatically from the final executable.
3. **Build Configuration:** Change your packaging Build Configuration to **Shipping** (or test once in **Development** first if you want access to logs, then do final verification in **Shipping**).
4. **Cook & Resolve Warnings:** Package for your target platform (e.g., Windows 64-bit). Read the Output Log carefully and eliminate compiler warnings, broken asset redirectors (run **Fix Up Redirectors** in the Content Browser), and missing asset references.
5. **Clean Environment Test:** Move the packaged build folder to a completely separate directory outside your Unreal project and run the `.exe` directly. Play through an entire loop—Main Menu -> Start Run -> Clear Rooms -> Pick Upgrades -> Defeat Boss -> Advance Floor -> Die -> Restart Game -> Verify Save Data Persisted—to confirm complete standalone stability.

